WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.320
Before Will Smith headed to the Wild Wild
West, he breezed right by The Matrix.

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:09.360
In a new video posted to his personal YouTube
channel, Smith dropped a bombshell: he turned

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.100
down the opportunity to star as Neo in The
Matrix.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.820
"I did turn down Neo in The Matrix."

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.859
Smith had previously admitted in a 2004 Wired
interview that he did turn down the role,

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:26.640
but in his new video, he finally gives the
full story and explains the circumstances

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.260
that pushed him to reject the offer.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.420
The actor rewound the clock to 1996 to tell
the tale from the very beginning.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.880
That year, Smith was wrapping up the final
season of The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air, the

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.210
sitcom series that put him on the map and
made him a household name, and was basking

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:45.580
in the sweet waters of success following the
release of Independence Day, which remains

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.100
one of the highest-grossing films in all of
cinema history.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.280
"Who's the man?

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:50.960
Who's the man?"

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.800
It's little wonder why Lana and Lilly Wachowski,
the writers and directors of The Matrix, wanted

00:00:55.810 --> 00:01:01.050
Smith to lead the film: he was gaining star
power by the second and seemed virtually untouchable.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.290
But Smith had clear-cut intentions for his
career moving forward, not wanting to isolate

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:09.200
himself to a certain kind of genre in apparent
fear of being type-cast.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:13.660
This led him to initially turn down Men in
Black two years prior to saying no to The

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:14.160
Matrix.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.740
"I had done Independence Day the year before,
so I was like, eh, I'm not doing another alien

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:19.240
movie.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:20.960
I don't want to be the alien movie guy.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:24.040
So I turned down Men in Black, and Steven
Spielberg called me.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.040
He was producing.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.930
He was like, 'Why are you turning down the
movie?'

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.290
I was like, 'You know, I just don't want to
be the alien guy.'"

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:33.180
He went on to say that he took Spielberg's
advice and eventually joined Men in Black

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.080
and felt on top of the world for a short while
after the film's debut in July of 1997.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.080
You just get in that universal energy, and
nothing can go wrong.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.500
But then the other side comes, and you can't
do nothing right."

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:51.220
Smith then brought us up to 1998, post-Men
in Black and pre-The Matrix, when he had some

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.520
big decisions to make.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:56.460
"So after we did Men in Black, the Wachowskis
came in, and it was like they had only done

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.280
one movie.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.240
[...] They came in and they made a pitch for
The Matrix, and it turns out, they're geniuses."

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.560
Still, Smith shared that the Wachowskis' idea
for The Matrix didn't strike his fancy.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:10.440
In fact, the Wachowskis' apparent laid back
behavior and strange pitch completely confused

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:10.940
Smith.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.340
Putting on a California surfer-type accent
to impersonate the Wachowskis, Smith described

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:15.880
the pitch:

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.480
"Imagine if you could stop jumping in the
middle of the jump … But then people could

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.900
see around you 360 [degrees] while you're
jumping,

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.920
"Nice to see an invention that actually works."

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.659
The Wachowskis' pitch for The Matrix ultimately
put Smith off the project.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:42.439
Instead, he made the steampunk Western action
comedy Wild Wild West — a low point in Smith's

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:43.340
professional life.

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:46.540
Still, Smith has no regrets about turning
down The Matrix.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.510
The actor feels he actually did movie-lovers
a great justice in rejecting the offer to

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.599
play Neo, admitting that he likely would have
screwed up The Matrix if he led the film and

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:55.599
not Keanu Reeves.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.299
"I probably would have messed The Matrix up.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:58.260
I would have ruined it.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.540
So I did y'all a favor."

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.629
Considering Smith already experienced wariness
at the thought of doing too many sci-fi action

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:08.540
movies back-to-back, he may not have given
the role of Neo the attention that it required.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.760
The actor was essentially prioritizing goofiness
over anything else back in the late 1990s.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:16.240
"And that would be my belt buckle.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.160
Gordon, when you're telling this story to
your grandkids, you make sure that you leave

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:20.760
this part out."

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.999
He very well could have taken a wildly different
approach to Neo than did Reeves, resulting

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.540
in a version of the character that was unable
to hit all the right emotional beats.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:32.680
As many Matrix fans will agree, no one but
the reserved, somewhat steely Reeves could

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:33.780
play Neo perfectly.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:36.920
"I know kung fu."

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.540
At the end of it all, as much as everyone,
Smith included, despised Wild Wild West, we're

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.700
glad Smith chose to make it over taking the
lead role in The Matrix.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.280
In a way, Smith stumbled in Wild Wild West
so Reeves could soar in The Matrix - and that

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.040
decision genuinely may have saved not just
the first film but also the entire franchise.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.120
"Now that's what I call a close encounter."

